00:14:59.530 --> 00:15:08.190
soispoke: SFI'd, it would be very, very nice to sort of, like, start making progress on the interaction between the two.

115
00:15:08.740 --> 00:15:12.920
soispoke: I think most of, what I…

116
00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:20.409
soispoke: Most of what my thoughts are, are, like, in this sort of, like, informational EIP for now.

117
00:15:20.770 --> 00:15:24.490
soispoke: So it's, EIP8369.

118
00:15:25.190 --> 00:15:44.910
soispoke: With the title that I maybe should have changed, it's called VOPS Profiles for FOCIL Eligibility, but, like, I think the crux of the CIP is to define what the interaction should be between FOCIL and prime transactions, really. Like, that's… the rest is mostly…

119
00:15:44.930 --> 00:15:59.319
soispoke: well known, like, so you have two profiles… two profiles. Profile one is, like, FOCIL as we know it, with, with sort of, like, regular transactions, and then profile two is, like, friend transactions, and how to sort of, like, dig with it.

120
00:16:00.860 --> 00:16:13.060
soispoke: And it does change, like, a ton of things, because instead of just, like, checking the notes and the balance now, all, you know, from transactions are much more expressive, and so…

121
00:16:13.160 --> 00:16:15.700
soispoke: You need to…

122
00:16:15.970 --> 00:16:25.620
soispoke: Yeah, sort of, like, check a lot more stuff than you need to, if you, if you have, regular transactions.

123
00:16:26.050 --> 00:16:37.340
soispoke: And… so the whole EIP, that's why it's, like, informational, and, like, it's… it sits in a bit of a weird place for now, because it's trying to describe interactions between,

124
00:16:37.950 --> 00:16:57.750
soispoke: You know, frame transactions and their shapes, and basically, it's all about the verify frame, and what is the load, inside the verifying frames, as it… as in, like, what state, it can read, what's the max verify guess limit, and all these things.

125
00:16:57.940 --> 00:17:05.240
soispoke: Then, you know, FOCIL, of course, and then VOPS, which is, like.

126
00:17:05.540 --> 00:17:12.930
soispoke: validity, only partial statelessness, and I think the easiest way to think about VOPS is just, like.

127
00:17:13.300 --> 00:17:19.989
soispoke: If we move towards a world in which we have any sort of, like, any kind of, like, partial statelessness.

128
00:17:20.170 --> 00:17:23.250
soispoke: And, you know, some nodes can drop.

129
00:17:23.380 --> 00:17:36.620
soispoke: most of the state and just keep the state that's needed to make sure, like, transactions are valid when you are, like, looking at them in the public mempool. Then we need to define

130
00:17:36.860 --> 00:17:45.080
soispoke: what, should be in that state. And we need to do it now, rather than later, I think, because

131
00:17:45.380 --> 00:17:51.799
soispoke: Applications that are building that are sort of, like, built on Ethereum now.

132
00:17:52.100 --> 00:18:07.809
soispoke: If… the idea is, like, if they build using state that is gonna be in VOPS, it is also gonna be… it's also gonna mean their transactions, are gonna benefit from FOCIL inclusion guarantees.

133
00:18:07.870 --> 00:18:27.489
soispoke: And so we need to give, you know, apps this sort of, like, guarantee, almost, that their transactions will be supported by FOCIL, because that's sort of, like, the minimal state we will ask Mempool nodes to hold, and that means, you know, transactions can go through the public mempool and benefit from FOCIL.

134
00:18:27.660 --> 00:18:32.360
soispoke: And so, yeah, in the EIP, we try to define,

135
00:18:32.570 --> 00:18:36.169
soispoke: The boundaries of that state,

136
00:18:36.980 --> 00:18:46.589
soispoke: It also ties in the privacy EIPs we've been working on, right? Because, for example, I would really want private transactions to be,

137
00:18:46.590 --> 00:18:57.829
soispoke: part of VOPS, and to benefit from FOCIL guarantees. So you have kid nunces in there, you have recent routines in there, so it's a very important part of the problem.

138
00:18:59.780 --> 00:19:16.560
soispoke: And so, yeah, defining that boundary is, like, very important, and I think it needs to be, like, a collaboration between client teams, right? Like, it can't just be sort of, like, arbitrarily determined by, just, like, an informational EIP, so I would really love, like, everyone

139
00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:20.789
soispoke: Of you guys to sort of, like, look into it if you have time, and…

140
00:19:20.790 --> 00:19:24.060
soispoke: And tell everyone if that makes sense or not, like…

141
00:19:24.080 --> 00:19:28.720
soispoke: You know, to include, like, these states in it, or not.

142
00:19:29.390 --> 00:19:35.880
soispoke: So, yeah, I think the state boundary is, like, very important, because… and the trade-off here is very obvious, right? It's, like.

143
00:19:36.130 --> 00:19:43.770
soispoke: Either include more states into VOPS, and then a broader range of transactions benefit from FOCIL,

144
00:19:44.070 --> 00:20:00.520
soispoke: which is great, of course, but that also means more state to hold 4 nodes, right? So that's the very obvious trade-off we have to sort of, like, deal with here. And so, depending on what we want for the network, we can either, like.

145
00:20:00.970 --> 00:20:08.919
soispoke: have a big VOPS, and then that means, you know, nodes will need to store weighted off state, but…

146
00:20:08.950 --> 00:20:25.990
soispoke: they can probably, sort of, like, accept, like, a lot more transactions can benefit from FOCIL, basically. Or you have a very small box, which is nice for… nice for decentralization, I guess, like, it's just, like, easier to run nodes, but, it means less, less transactions benefit from FOCIL.

147
00:20:26.850 --> 00:20:32.050
soispoke: So that's one key dimension I wanted to talk about, and then…

148
00:20:32.800 --> 00:20:39.289
soispoke: I think the second important dimension is, like, the whole mechanics of how to

149
00:20:39.650 --> 00:20:57.399
soispoke: check, you know, how to do the FOCIL checks, basically, like, in the normal FOCIL, like a regular FOCIL, let's say, let's call it, like, you just happen the transaction at the end of the payload to… to see if it's valid, and… and here you can't really do that with SRAM transactions, so…

150
00:20:57.680 --> 00:21:17.309
soispoke: we thought of the builder index sort of, like, mechanism, and that's, that's also something I would love to have some more feedback on, like, does it make sense? And… and I think we also need to get into the technicalities of, like, you know, how that builder index is communicated, and,

151
00:21:17.680 --> 00:21:18.650
soispoke: what…

152
00:21:19.010 --> 00:21:30.720
soispoke: like, yeah, is it part of, like, an existing object? Is it, like, an object on its own? And so, yeah, we'll probably need to settle on this.

153
00:21:31.020 --> 00:21:36.849
soispoke: So I guess that's, like, the other sort of, like, important part, for me, and then…

154
00:21:37.530 --> 00:21:49.559
soispoke: Yeah, more generally, I think, like, as a coordination objective, like, it would be really nice to settle towards, like, some kind of specs for certain frames.

155
00:21:50.150 --> 00:22:03.200
soispoke: And then there's this sort of, like, yeah, thing we also need to settle of, like, whether that lives in existing FOCIL and prime EIPs. I think it should, I think it actually should live in both.

156
00:22:03.480 --> 00:22:18.610
soispoke: But, you know, we need, like, to… that means we need to update FOCIL itself, we need to update, frame transactions, probably as well, and… and so there… there will need to be quite a lot of coordination around this interaction, and…

157
00:22:18.760 --> 00:22:28.330
soispoke: And, I think, like, the whole… all the concepts are sort of, like, settled. Like, I don't feel like there is any sort of, like, huge questions that are, like, we haven't solved or anything.

158
00:22:28.490 --> 00:22:33.619
soispoke: But now, you know, when we… when we go from concepts to implementations, we…

159
00:22:33.970 --> 00:22:43.899
soispoke: we get into, like, a lot of, technical things we need to discuss and sort of, so… yeah, I want to… I would want to bring this up, basically.

160
00:22:44.370 --> 00:22:46.890
soispoke: What more can I say?

161
00:22:49.380 --> 00:22:56.069
